  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 212 PM EDT Tuesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Mostly dry and seasonable conditions are \nexpected to continue across the region through the remainder of \nthe week, with a few chances for showers possible. High \ntemperatures will generally be in the 70s to low 80s the next \nseveral days. Another round of patchy fog will be possible \ntonight into tomorrow morning with high pressure overhead, but \nmoisture will be limited which will influence how much fog \ndevelops overnight. A decaying cold front will move through the \nregion tomorrow, bringing chances for scattered showers and \npossible embedded thunderstorms, particularly across northern \nNew York. Showers and any thunderstorms that do develop look to \nbe rather unimpactful, with limited dynamics and instability to \npromote any stronger thunderstorms. Shower chances may linger \ninto the evening, especially across the higher terrain before \ndrier conditions return to the region. Some showers cannot be \nentirely ruled out on Thursday as a weak area of low pressure \nmoving across southern New England, but chances are fairly low \nand dry conditions are expected to prevail across most of the \narea, with dry and seasonable conditions continuing into Friday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: A deep 500 millibar trough is expected to dig \ninto the Great Lakes region Saturday night, leading to \nwidespread showers overspreading our area into Sunday. Expect \nshowers will tend to be orographically enhanced with this setup,\nbut thankfully there are are no signals for noteworthy moisture\nor heat to advect northward, which limits potential for \nimpactful/hazardous weather. As such, the latest 48 hour \nrainfall probabilities of exceeding 1.00\" of rain are only in \nthe 15 - 40% range with 2.00\" probabilities below 10%. Greatest \nchances of the higher rainfall amounts appear primarily in the \nwestern foothills of the Adirondacks and westward into the St. \nLawrence Valley. Given the aforementioned meridional winds and \nlikely deep upper level low, would expect a fairly long duration\nevent with at least periods of showers. Generally this system \nshould bring plenty of cloud cover and cool air from the \nnorthwest by Monday, such that daytime temperatures will trend \ntemporarily below normal before recovering towards midweek \nfollowing the departure of the trough.",
